LEAD = {
    "headline": "A LINGERIE BRAND HELD 3.14 MILLION PEOPLE ON ITS OWN YOUTUBE CHANNEL AT ONCE ON SUNDAY NIGHT AND THE EVENT PEAK ROSE ALMOST A THIRD ON LAST YEAR, AFTER IT RAN A REALITY SERIES ON THE SAME CHANNEL FIRST",
    "deck": "The Victoria's Secret Fashion Show streamed on Sunday and the audience data landed this morning.  Streams Charts puts it in the year's ten most popular non-sports livestreams, which is rare company for a brand's own channel, and the two decisions that preceded it both cut against how owned video normally gets planned.  One narrowed the distribution.  The other spent the money before the event rather than on it.",
    "stamps": [
        ("STREAMS CHARTS · 20 OCT", "https://streamscharts.com/news/victorias-secret-fashion-show-2026-viewership"),
        ("THE PUBLISH PRESS · 19 OCT", "https://news.thepublishpress.com/p/victoria-s-secret-makes-underwear-cool-again"),
        ("YOUTUBE BLOG · 30 SEP", "https://blog.youtube/culture-and-trends/watch-victorias-secret-fashion-show-2026-youtube/"),
        ("VARIETY · 12 OCT", "https://variety.com/2026/tv/global/rtl-touts-youtube-cobra-11-rise-subscriptions-1236908811/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Two terms first, because the whole item turns on them and sellers swap them constantly.  Peak concurrents is the largest number of people watching at the same moment — one snapshot, the busiest second of the night.  Hours watched is everyone's viewing time added together, which means it rises when a show simply runs longer or when more channels carry it.  Peak tells you how big the room got.  Hours tells you how long people stayed in it, and nothing about how crowded it was.",
        "The show ran on Sunday at Sony Pictures Studios in Culver City.  Streams Charts published the figures this morning: <mark>3.64 million peak concurrents across every tracked livestream, and 3.14 million of those on the official Victoria's Secret YouTube channel alone</mark>, which is a record for that channel.  Across all platforms the event generated <mark>4.8 million hours watched, 81.72 percent of it on the brand's own channel</mark>.  The event's total peak was up <mark>32.9 percent on last year</mark>, and this is the first year the show has cleared three million people watching at once.  A total of 113 unique broadcasting channels covered it, the brand's own PINK line among them, and YouTube carried over 99 percent of the livestreams.  Exactly one Twitch stream carried it, a watch party by Evelyn Ortiz.",
        "Now the first decision.  Last year the show was split across Amazon Live, Prime Video and assorted social platforms.  This year the brand put the primary livestream on YouTube and left it there.  Fewer places to watch, more people watching.  Be careful with that sentence, because Streams Charts does not isolate the platform change from anything else that happened, and a year is a long time for other things to happen.  What you can say is that consolidating did not cost the brand an audience, which is the opposite of what a multi-platform plan is sold on.",
        "The second decision is the one worth copying.  In the weeks before the runway the brand ran a reality series on the same channel, Angels Among Us, in which women competed for a place in the actual show.  The Publish Press reports more than 100,000 applicants and almost 19 million views across the series by the day after the broadcast.  So the live event arrived with a cast the audience already knew and a result it already cared about.  It was the last episode of something rather than a show on its own, and the series idea came out of the comment section — chief executive Hillary Super says the brand took it from what customers were asking for in comments and on social.",
        "The same mechanism turned up at MIPCOM in Cannes last week from a direction nobody expects, and it is worth holding next to this.  Reported by Variety on 12 October: RTL Deutschland chief executive Stephan Schmitter, on stage with YouTube's vice president for Europe, said the German broadcaster had uploaded <mark>more than 200 full episodes of Cobra 11 — an Autobahn police action series running since 1996 — to YouTube a year ago and drawn 30 million views</mark>, 83 percent of those viewers men, average age 30.  He says the show had little commercial value left after being rerun to death on linear and streaming, and credits the YouTube run with a <mark>123 percent increase in first video views for the series on the paid service RTL+ and a 26 percent rise in subscriptions linked to the show</mark>.  His own description of the mechanism is as plain as it gets: they discover or rediscover the show, and if they want new episodes, more flying cars and explosions, they have to go to RTL+.",
        "Put the three together and the shape is the same every time.  The free owned video is the acquisition media.  The gated thing — a subscription, a product, a ticket, a runway show — is what gets paid for.  In none of these cases is the owned channel being run as advertising inventory to sell, which is how most brand channels are still justified internally, and in all three the spend that did the work landed before the moment everyone measures.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("3.14M", "people on the brand's own YouTube channel at the same moment on Sunday night"),
        ("32.9%", "growth in the event's total peak audience on last year, the first time it has cleared three million"),
        ("113", "separate channels carried the stream, including the brand's own PINK line"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Streams Charts is a commercial analytics company that sells this data and sells brand-mention tracking alongside it, and its figures for peak concurrents and hours watched are its own measurement rather than a platform disclosure.  The 32.9 percent growth figure is for the event's total peak across every stream; Streams Charts calls the 3.14 million a record for the brand's channel but publishes no year-on-year figure for that channel alone.  The applicant and series view figures are the brand's own, reported by The Publish Press, with no measurement provider named, and no cost figure has been published for the series, so nothing here tells you what the audience was bought for.  Nobody has published an advertising or sponsorship revenue figure for the stream either, so do not read the absence of named sponsors in the coverage as proof that none were sold.  On the RTL side, Schmitter is selling a decision he made, on a stage he shared with YouTube's European sales leadership, and the 26 percent is described as linked to the show rather than isolated against a control, so it is not clean incremental growth and he did not present it as such.",
    "so_what": "The live event is the part that gets funded because it is the part that photographs, and on these numbers it is the payoff rather than the asset.  What produced a record concurrent audience was weeks of cheap serialised content on the same channel giving people a reason to have a side, plus a decision to stop spreading the broadcast across platforms that each took a slice.  The RTL case is the same trade with the free and paid halves swapped: a dead archive became the cheapest audience acquisition the broadcaster owns, precisely because it was already made and already paid for.",
    "do_this": "Before you commit next year's tentpole budget, carve out the four to six weeks before it and commission serialised content on your own channel that makes the event have a winner, a cast or a result.  Then pick one platform for the live stream and cut the simulcast, and check in your own analytics whether last year's second and third platforms actually delivered an audience you could not have reached on the first.  And go through your back catalogue this week for finished video you are sitting on — old series, old long-form, old campaign films — and put it up free, because it is the only inventory you own that costs nothing to make.",
}

